# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Mission Control — environment
#
# Nothing here is required to run the dashboard. With an empty .env the app
# boots on PGlite with seeded data and every agent runs in simulate mode.
# Fill these in as you unlock capability; see SETUP_TODO.md for what each one
# actually unblocks.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# --- Storage -----------------------------------------------------------------
# Unset  -> local PGlite database at ./data/mission-control  (default, zero setup)
# Set    -> any Postgres (Supabase / Neon / self-hosted). Same migrations.
#           Use the Session pooler string from Supabase, not db.<ref>.supabase.co.
# DATABASE_URL=postgresql://user:pass@host:5432/postgres

# --- Agent runtime -----------------------------------------------------------
# Unset -> every agent runs in simulate mode: real activity + memory rows are
#          written and the tree updates, but no model is called.
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# Hard ceiling on what a single agent run may cost, in USD. Runs that would
# exceed it stop and post a blocker instead.
AGENT_MAX_COST_USD=0.50

# --- Chat channel (§6) -------------------------------------------------------
# TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=
# TELEGRAM_CHAT_ID=
# TELEGRAM_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# --- Scheduler (§7) ----------------------------------------------------------
# Unset -> /api/tick returns 503 and the cadence table never fires on its own.
#          `npm run tick` still works and is unaffected.
# Set    -> /api/tick runs whatever is due. Vercel Cron sends this as a bearer
#          token automatically; any other scheduler sends x-cron-secret.
#          Generate one with:  openssl rand -hex 32
# CRON_SECRET=

# Voice notes. Anthropic has no speech-to-text endpoint, so this is the one
# place another provider is called. Unset -> voice notes reply that they could
# not be transcribed; typed commands are unaffected.
# OPENAI_API_KEY=

# --- Integrations (§8) -------------------------------------------------------
# All optional. An agent whose source is missing reports
# "kaynak kullanilamiyor" rather than inventing a number.

# Lead discovery. Places bills by which fields you request, not just by request
# count, so the field mask in src/lib/integrations/places.ts is the minimum both
# ICPs need — widen it deliberately.
# Untouched leads are deleted after 30 days; see RETENTION_DAYS.
# GOOGLE_PLACES_API_KEY=

# Cold email. Nothing is ever sent unattended — outreach_send is gated
# unconditionally, so every message waits for an approval on the phone first.
# Only fires on approval when the channel is "email"; every other channel
# (Instagram DM, etc.) still lands in the approval card but stays manual —
# no provider is wired for those.
# RESEND_API_KEY=
#
# One sending address per branch, on its own domain, not the operational one
# (n8n, webmail, this dashboard) — see DEPLOY.md for why. A name-shaped local
# part reads less like mass mail than "outreach@" or "noreply@" and tends to
# get more replies; adjust to taste.
# RESEND_FROM_WEB=ates@atesdesign.agency
# RESEND_FROM_AUTOMATION=ates@atesflow.agency
#
# Inbound replies. Turn on "Enable receiving" for the domain in Resend, point
# its webhook at https://<your-host>/api/resend, and paste the signing secret
# it gives you here. Never triggers a reply — a reply is written to the Brain
# (client-scoped, so an agent has it as context) and pushed to Telegram; the
# owner still writes and sends the reply by hand, same as everything else.
# RESEND_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# Today's calls in the brief. A secret-address .ics feed — in Google Calendar:
# Settings → your calendar → "Secret address in iCal format". No OAuth needed.
# Read-only: it can list the day, it cannot book anything. Leave it set even
# after wiring OAuth below and it stays as the fallback if the token ever dies.
# CALENDAR_URL=

# Booking, moving and cancelling meetings, with a Google Meet link and an invite
# Google mails to the attendees itself. This is the write half of the calendar
# and needs a real OAuth client — DEPLOY.md → "Takvim ve Meet" walks the ~20
# minutes of console work, then `npm run google:auth` prints the refresh token.
#
# Nothing on the calendar happens unattended: creating, changing and cancelling
# are all behind an unconditional approval, so every one of them lands on the
# phone first and is reported back afterwards with the Meet link.
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=
# GOOGLE_REFRESH_TOKEN=
#
# Which calendar to write to. Unset means the account's primary calendar, which
# is what you want unless the agency has its own — then paste that calendar's ID
# from Google Calendar → settings → "Takvim kimliği".
# GOOGLE_CALENDAR_ID=

# Lighthouse runs through Google PageSpeed Insights. It works without a key at
# low volume; the shared keyless quota runs out fast, and a 429 makes the
# auditor report "could not measure" rather than guess.
# Free key: https://developers.google.com/speed/docs/insights/v5/get-started
# GOOGLE_PAGESPEED_API_KEY=

# --- Kur ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# No key, nothing to set. TL amounts are converted through TCMB's daily rate
# XML; the source is the `fx.source` setting ("tcmb" or "erapi"), changeable by
# telling the manager on Telegram. An unreachable source converts nothing and
# says so — it never falls back to a guessed rate.
